// Pack two reals into one memory, as a complex number.
//
// PRGM has seven memories, and a complex number is two reals — so in CMPLX mode
// one memory can hold a *pair* of values. That is what this library is for: when
// a program needs more live values than it has memories, pack pairs into complex
// numbers and unpack them where they are used.
//
//     let p = pack(x, y);      // one memory: x + yi
//     print(unpack_x(p));      // the x back
//     print(unpack_y(p));      // the y back
//
// `examples/packing.fxc` uses it to keep eight reals live in four memories,
// which the same program cannot do unpacked.
//
// ## Unpacking needs `conjg`, because there is no `ReP`/`ImP`
//
// The real and imaginary parts are recovered with arithmetic identities, since
// this model has no "real part" or "imaginary part" key — `ReP`/`ImP` are a
// documented gap (see the TODO list in `docs/LANGUAGE.md`):
//
//     z + conjg(z) = 2·x        x = (z + conjg(z)) / 2
//     z - conjg(z) = 2·y·i      y = (z - conjg(z)) / (2i)
//
// Both are exact *arithmetically*: adding and subtracting a conjugate pair
// leaves only the doubled part, and dividing by two is exact (a power of two).
// The one place a rounding can creep in is the doubling, because `2x` may need
// one digit more than `x` did and the machine normalises every operation to 15
// significant digits. So the round trip is guaranteed for
//
//   * a value of **up to 14 significant digits** — `unpack_x(pack(x, y))` is
//     exactly `x`; measured over 1200 random pairs at 10, 12, 13 and 14 digits,
//     with no failures;
//   * a **15-digit** value may lose its last digit, because `2x` needs 16 —
//     measured at 139 of 200 pairs.
//
// The display shows 10 significant digits, so this is invisible in practice:
// packing is lossless for everything the machine can print. It is not lossless
// for every `f64`, and it is better to say so than to imply otherwise — the
// difference is a last-place rounding, not an error that grows.
//
// ## What packing costs, and when to use it
//
// Packing trades **program bytes for memories**, and only the second is scarce
// in the way this helps with. Every call is inlined, so each `unpack_x(p)` emits
// the six keys of `(p+Conjg(p))÷2` and each `pack(x, y)` the four of `x+yi`. So:
//
//   * pack a pair when a *memory* is what you are short of, not when the program
//     is too long — it always makes the program longer;
//   * unpack straight into the expression that needs the value
//     (`print(unpack_x(p))`) rather than into a new variable, which would spend
//     the memory you just saved;
//   * `free` the two reals as soon as they are packed, or they still occupy
//     two memories each and the packing has bought nothing.
//
// Arithmetic on the packed value works on both parts at once, which is the other
// half of the point: one `+` adds two coordinates, and `abs(p)` is the distance
// from the origin with nothing unpacked.
//
// ## The mode
//
// A library cannot declare `#mode`, so the **program that includes this must say
// `#mode CMPLX`** — `i()` and `conjg` are CMPLX keys and are rejected in COMP.

/// Combine two reals into one complex value.
fn pack(x, y) = x + y * i();

/// The first of the two values packed by [`pack`].
fn unpack_x(p) = (p + conjg(p)) / 2;

/// The second of the two values packed by [`pack`].
fn unpack_y(p) = (p - conjg(p)) / (2 * i());
